  "query_preview": "..The large-scale pattern this evening consists of a\nclosed low centered over northwest Arizona while upstream, \nanother closed low is located off the southern BritCol coast. The\nlow over Arizona resulted in some isolated to scattered showers \nover portions of southern and eastern Utah earlier today, with \nmuch of this having already decreased this evening. However, a \nvort lobe embedded within the low will rotate into southeast Utah \ntonight. This is expected to bring an increasing threat of \nshowers with a few embedded thunderstorms back to southern and \neastern Utah, mainly after midnight. As the rest of the upper low\nlifts through the Four Corners area through the day Sunday, \nexpect generally scattered showers and thunderstorms to continue \nover roughly the spine of Utah and points east. Can not rule out\nan isolated flash flood with stronger showers moving over a \nsensitive basin.\n\nA relative lull in precipitation is expected for Monday as a\ntransient shortwave ridge crosses the area. Temperatures on\nMonday will trend warmer under the ridge, with northern Utah\nreturning closer to normal while southern Utah will remain below\nnormal. \n\nMeanwhile, the closed low over the BritCol coast will move inland\nacross the PacNW Sunday night into Monday before grazing northern\nUtah Monday night as a fast-moving shortwave trough. This trough \nwill push a relatively shallow cold front into northern Utah \nMonday night. This will drop temperatures across northern Utah to \ngenerally 4-8 degrees below normal for Tuesday. Generally light \nprecipitation is expected near the cold front, though amounts will\ndepend in part on the strength of the cold front, of which subtle\nchanges could make a noticeable difference. The latest \ndeterministic GFS and NAM are leaning towards a weaker front with \nlower QPF compared to the EC. The 19z NBM has 25th-75th percentile\nvalues of around 0.15-0.6 in the northern Wasatch Mountains and \nBear River Range (highest along the Idaho border), and from less \nthan 0.05-0.4 inches in the adjacent valleys. Snow levels look to \nfall to between 6-7kft, so light snow accumulations can be \nexpected in the mountains. \n\nA near zonal flow pattern is expected to develop on Tuesday before\nsouthwesterly flow develops on Wednesday as a weakening and\nrelatively benign trough grazes northern Utah. Thereafter, another\nstorm system will reach the PacNW coast by Thursday. This will tap\ninto some moisture and bring potentially widespread precipitation\nback to the area as early as Friday and continuing through the\nweekend. However, the details on how this storm system will evolve\n(whether it splits, and how quickly it moves through the area,\netc) are still pending better model agreement.",
